======================================================================================================================
g04a -- the cluster source: its required phase-space density, and what that admits
======================================================================================================================

  R1  the source the corrected data require (seven clusters with measured stellar profiles, median):
       r [kpc]  M_b [1e12]  M_src [1e12]  M_src/M_b  rho_src [Msun/kpc^3]  rho_src [kg/m^3]
            40       0.504         3.573       7.09             5.332e+06         3.609e-22
            50       0.713         4.645       6.51             3.704e+06         2.508e-22
            75       1.209         8.128       6.72             2.412e+06         1.633e-22
           100       1.881        13.171       7.00             1.740e+06         1.178e-22
           150       3.728        25.793       6.92             9.744e+05         6.596e-23
           200       6.015        40.721       6.77             6.360e+05         4.306e-23
           300      11.650        76.917       6.60             3.686e+05         2.496e-23
           420      19.614       134.864       6.88             1.983e+05         1.343e-23
           750      45.632       240.689       5.27             4.537e+04         3.071e-24
  [PASS] R1 [requirement] the corrected X-COP profiles require an extra source of five to eight times the baryons over 40-750 kpc, with a well-defined density profile that any candidate must reproduce   (M_src/M_b = 5.3-7.1; central density 3.61e-22 kg/m^3 at 40 kpc)

  R2  the SAME component must be nearly absent in galaxies, where the ceiling holds for 99% of points.
      cluster 1-D dispersion (kT = 5 keV): 886 km/s;  galaxy: 150 km/s
      in galaxies the ceiling caps the excess at C a0 = 0.6476 a0; a baryon-tracing source of ratio f would add g_src = f g_bar,
      so at the transition g_bar ~ a0 the ceiling allows f <= 0.648, i.e. under 65% of the baryons, against 6.8x in clusters.
  [PASS] R2 [contrast] the required source must be present at several times the baryons in clusters and at under one times them in galaxies: a contrast of at least an order of magnitude between the two environments, which is the real constraint on any candidate   (required contrast in M_src/M_b between cluster cores and galaxies: >= 10x)

  R3  Tremaine-Gunn: rho_max = (2 pi)^{3/2} g_s m^4 sigma^3 / h^3.  Validation against the textbook case:
      a 1 eV fermion in a dwarf spheroidal (sigma = 10 km/s) can reach at most 1.09e-30 kg/m^3 = 1.61e-02 Msun/kpc^3,
      far below a dwarf's ~0.1 Msun/pc^3 = 1.0e+08 Msun/kpc^3 -- which is why light fermions are excluded from dwarfs, the standard result the formula must reproduce.
      the source needs rho = 3.609e-22 kg/m^3 at 40 kpc where sigma = 886 km/s
      => any fermionic relic supplying it must have m >= 4.67 eV
  [PASS] R3 [phase space] the formula reproduces the standard exclusion of light fermions from dwarf spheroidals, and applied to the cluster core it sets a hard lower bound on the mass of any fermionic relic that could be the source   (validation: 1 eV in a dwarf reaches only 1.6e-02 Msun/kpc^3; cluster core requires m >= 4.67 eV)

  R4  the light-relic option, priced.  A thermal relic of mass m contributes Omega h^2 = m/(93.14 eV) per species.
      m =   1.00 eV: Omega h^2 = 0.0107 (Omega = 0.024);  max mass inside 100 kpc at the cluster dispersion = 4.71e+10 Msun, required 1.32e+13 (0.00x)
      m =   2.00 eV: Omega h^2 = 0.0215 (Omega = 0.048);  max mass inside 100 kpc at the cluster dispersion = 7.53e+11 Msun, required 1.32e+13 (0.06x)
      m =   5.00 eV: Omega h^2 = 0.0537 (Omega = 0.119);  max mass inside 100 kpc at the cluster dispersion = 2.94e+13 Msun, required 1.32e+13 (2.23x)
      m =   4.67 eV: Omega h^2 = 0.0501 (Omega = 0.111);  max mass inside 100 kpc at the cluster dispersion = 2.23e+13 Msun, required 1.32e+13 (1.70x)
  [PASS] R4 [hot relics] a relic light enough to be a plausible thermal species cannot fill the cluster CORE: at 2 eV the phase-space ceiling allows only a fraction of the mass the inner 100 kpc requires, so the classic light-neutrino fix fails on the core, not on the total   (a 2 eV fermion supplies at most 0.06 of the mass required inside 100 kpc; the bound needs m >= 4.67 eV, at which Omega h^2 = 0.050)

  R4b the shape test, which the phase-space floor makes unavoidable.  A relic AT its Tremaine-Gunn limit has a
      CONSTANT-density core, so its enclosed ratio to the baryons rises -- the same failure as the framework's dust.
      the required source density runs as rho ~ r^(-1.53) over 40-750 kpc, so it is NOT cored: to reproduce that at
      40 kpc the relic must sit well BELOW its phase-space ceiling there.  Demanding a factor 10 of headroom:
        headroom   1.0x -> m >=   4.67 eV,  Omega h^2 = 0.0501  (Omega = 0.110 at h = 0.674), thermal free-streaming ~ 4.3 Mpc
        headroom   3.0x -> m >=   6.14 eV,  Omega h^2 = 0.0660  (Omega = 0.145 at h = 0.674), thermal free-streaming ~ 3.3 Mpc
        headroom  10.0x -> m >=   8.30 eV,  Omega h^2 = 0.0891  (Omega = 0.196 at h = 0.674), thermal free-streaming ~ 2.4 Mpc
  [PASS] R4b [shape] a relic sitting at its phase-space floor is cored and so fails the same shape requirement the framework's dust fails; escaping that pushes the mass to roughly 8 eV and above, where the species carries most of the dark-matter budget and is no longer a light relic at all   (required rho ~ r^(-1.53), not cored; a factor 10 of phase-space headroom needs m >= 8.3 eV, i.e. Omega = 0.20 of a total Omega_dm = 0.26)

  R5  the framework's own dark sector (g03r): rho_d ~ exp(-r/H)/g, so its ENCLOSED ratio to the baryons rises outward,
      while the data require a ratio that is flat or falling (g03u B8: log-slope -0.05 over 40-750 kpc).
      measured requirement: d log(M_src/M_b)/d log r = -0.05
      CORRECTION (g04b, and the lead's corrected atmosphere comparison): this requirement is NOT decisive against the dust.
      Fitted to the corrected profiles the atmosphere reaches 0.186 dex rms at |K_2| = 2.0e5, inside the KiDS/cluster window;
      the corrected required source is not core-heavy either (it peaks near 100 kpc).  What remains is a factor-3 offset in
      the peak radius (model ~300 kpc vs data ~100 kpc), which is a discrepancy, not an exclusion.
  [PASS] R5 [the dust, CORRECTED] the framework's dark sector is NOT excluded on shape: fitted to the corrected profiles it reaches 0.186 dex rms inside its own window, and the outstanding discrepancy is a factor-3 offset in the radius where the ratio peaks   (required log-slope -0.05 over 40-750 kpc; the atmosphere's best fit is 0.186 dex rms at |K_2| = 2.0e5 with its peak at ~300 kpc against the data's ~100 kpc)

  R6  what any candidate must satisfy, all four at once:
      (a) supply 6.8x the baryons inside 400 kpc, with a density of 3.61e-22 kg/m^3 at 40 kpc;
      (b) have a phase-space density allowing that at sigma = 886 km/s, i.e. m >= 4.67 eV if a fermionic relic;
      (c) be absent in galaxies at the level the ceiling permits, a contrast of >= 10x in M_src/M_b;
      (d) have an enclosed ratio to the baryons that is flat or falling, not rising.
      A light thermal relic fails (a)+(b) in the core, and one heavy enough to pass
      them is no longer light: it must sit well above its phase-space floor, which pushes it to ~8 eV and most of the dark-matter budget.
      The framework's own condensate dust, however, is NOT excluded: see g04b, where the atmosphere fitted to the corrected
      profiles reaches 0.186 dex rms inside its own window, leaving a factor-3 offset in the peak radius as the discrepancy.
      Among RELICS, what satisfies all four is a cold component; but Tremaine-Gunn does not constrain a condensate, so the
      framework's own dust is untouched by this script and is settled in g04b instead: not excluded, a 0.186 dex match.
  [PASS] R6 [verdict, CORRECTED] the four requirements exclude LIGHT RELICS on phase space, which is what this script establishes; they do NOT exclude the framework's condensate, whose pressure is not collisionless and which g04b shows reaches 0.186 dex rms inside its own window   (a light relic fails the core phase-space requirement below m = 4.67 eV; the condensate is not constrained by Tremaine-Gunn and is not excluded)

  caveats: hydrostatic equilibrium is assumed for the source requirement and tested only in g03u; the Tremaine-Gunn bound is
  the collisionless-fermion limit and does not constrain a condensate, a bosonic field or a dissipative component; sigma is taken
  from kT = 5 keV with mu = 0.61 rather than from a measured dispersion profile; the density profile is differenced from nine
  tabulated radii, so its innermost value is the coarsest.  total 0s

RESULT: 0 FAIL
